In classic World of Warcraft, most warlocks are seen with Voidwalkers because they are the most reliable and versatile pet for solo leveling and questing. While the Succubus offers higher damage and crowd control, the Voidwalker’s ability to generate threat and tank damage makes it much easier to manage multiple enemies and survive difficult encounters.

The Voidwalker’s skills—Torment, Suffering, and Sacrifice—allow it to hold aggro and protect the warlock, which is crucial for solo play. In contrast, the Succubus is more fragile and requires more precise positioning and crowd control management, making it less forgiving for general questing. Additionally, the Voidwalker’s out-of-combat health regeneration helps sustain the warlock during long questing sessions.

Ultimately, the Voidwalker is the "default" choice because it provides a safety net and consistent tanking, whereas the Succubus is a more specialized tool for specific situations. [end of text]


